{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A query function can be literally any function that "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"returns a promise"}]},{"type":"text","value":". The promise that is returned should either "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"resolve the data"}]},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"throw an error"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All of the following are valid query function configurations:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useQuery(['todos'], fetchAllTodos)\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const data = await fetchTodoById(todoId)\n  return data\n})\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], ({ queryKey }) => fetchTodoById(queryKey[1]))","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"handling-and-throwing-errors","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Handling and Throwing Errors"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For React Query to determine a query has errored, the query function "},{"type":"strong","children":[{"type":"text","value":"must throw"}]},{"type":"text","value":". Any error that is thrown in the query function will be persisted on the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"error"},{"type":"text","value":" state of the query."}]},{"type":"code","value":"const { error } = useQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  if (somethingGoesWrong) {\n    throw new Error('Oh no!')\n  }\n\n  return data\n})","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"usage-with-fetch-and-other-clients-that-do-not-throw-by-default","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Usage with "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" and other clients that do not throw by default"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"While most utilities like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"axios"},{"type":"text","value":" or "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"graphql-request"},{"type":"text","value":" automatically throw errors for unsuccessful HTTP calls, some utilities like "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" do not throw errors by default. If that's the case, you'll need to throw them on your own. Here is a simple way to do that with the popular "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"fetch"},{"type":"text","value":" API:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"useQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const response = await fetch('/todos/' + todoId)\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error('Network response was not ok')\n  }\n  return response.json()\n})","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"query-function-variables","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query Function Variables"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Query keys are not just for uniquely identifying the data you are fetching, but are also conveniently passed into your query function and while not always necessary, this makes it possible to extract your query functions if needed:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"function Todos({ status, page }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', { status, page }], fetchTodoList)\n}\n\n// Access the key, status and page variables in your query function!\nfunction fetchTodoList({ queryKey }) {\n  const [_key, { status, page }] = queryKey\n  return new Promise()\n}","lang":"js"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"using-a-query-object-instead-of-parameters","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Using a Query Object instead of parameters"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Anywhere the "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"[queryKey, queryFn, config]"},{"type":"text","value":" signature is supported throughout React Query's API, you can also use an object to express the same configuration:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { useQuery } from 'react-query'\n\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: ['todo', 7],\n  queryFn: fetchTodo,\n  ...config,\n})","lang":"js"}],"headings":[{"id":"handling-and-throwing-errors","text":"Handling and Throwing Errors","level":2},{"id":"usage-with-fetch-and-other-clients-that-do-not-throw-by-default","text":"Usage with fetch and other clients that do not throw by default","level":2},{"id":"query-function-variables","text":"Query Function Variables","level":2},{"id":"using-a-query-object-instead-of-parameters","text":"Using a Query Object instead of parameters","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nA query function can be literally any function that **returns a promise**. The promise that is returned should either **resolve the data** or **throw an error**.\n\nAll of the following are valid query function configurations:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos'], fetchAllTodos)\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const data = await fetchTodoById(todoId)\n  return data\n})\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], ({ queryKey }) => fetchTodoById(queryKey[1]))\n```\n\n## Handling and Throwing Errors\n\nFor React Query to determine a query has errored, the query function **must throw**. Any error that is thrown in the query function will be persisted on the `error` state of the query.\n\n```js\nconst { error } = useQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  if (somethingGoesWrong) {\n    throw new Error('Oh no!')\n  }\n\n  return data\n})\n```\n\n## Usage with `fetch` and other clients that do not throw by default\n\nWhile most utilities like `axios` or `graphql-request` automatically throw errors for unsuccessful HTTP calls, some utilities like `fetch` do not throw errors by default. If that's the case, you'll need to throw them on your own. Here is a simple way to do that with the popular `fetch` API:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const response = await fetch('/todos/' + todoId)\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error('Network response was not ok')\n  }\n  return response.json()\n})\n```\n\n## Query Function Variables\n\nQuery keys are not just for uniquely identifying the data you are fetching, but are also conveniently passed into your query function and while not always necessary, this makes it possible to extract your query functions if needed:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos({ status, page }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', { status, page }], fetchTodoList)\n}\n\n// Access the key, status and page variables in your query function!\nfunction fetchTodoList({ queryKey }) {\n  const [_key, { status, page }] = queryKey\n  return new Promise()\n}\n```\n\n## Using a Query Object instead of parameters\n\nAnywhere the `[queryKey, queryFn, config]` signature is supported throughout React Query's API, you can also use an object to express the same configuration:\n\n```js\nimport { useQuery } from 'react-query'\n\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: ['todo', 7],\n  queryFn: fetchTodo,\n  ...config,\n})\n```\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\nid: query-functions\ntitle: Query Functions\n---\n\nA query function can be literally any function that **returns a promise**. The promise that is returned should either **resolve the data** or **throw an error**.\n\nAll of the following are valid query function configurations:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos'], fetchAllTodos)\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], () => fetchTodoById(todoId))\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const data = await fetchTodoById(todoId)\n  return data\n})\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], ({ queryKey }) => fetchTodoById(queryKey[1]))\n```\n\n## Handling and Throwing Errors\n\nFor React Query to determine a query has errored, the query function **must throw**. Any error that is thrown in the query function will be persisted on the `error` state of the query.\n\n```js\nconst { error } = useQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  if (somethingGoesWrong) {\n    throw new Error('Oh no!')\n  }\n\n  return data\n})\n```\n\n## Usage with `fetch` and other clients that do not throw by default\n\nWhile most utilities like `axios` or `graphql-request` automatically throw errors for unsuccessful HTTP calls, some utilities like `fetch` do not throw errors by default. If that's the case, you'll need to throw them on your own. Here is a simple way to do that with the popular `fetch` API:\n\n```js\nuseQuery(['todos', todoId], async () => {\n  const response = await fetch('/todos/' + todoId)\n  if (!response.ok) {\n    throw new Error('Network response was not ok')\n  }\n  return response.json()\n})\n```\n\n## Query Function Variables\n\nQuery keys are not just for uniquely identifying the data you are fetching, but are also conveniently passed into your query function and while not always necessary, this makes it possible to extract your query functions if needed:\n\n```js\nfunction Todos({ status, page }) {\n  const result = useQuery(['todos', { status, page }], fetchTodoList)\n}\n\n// Access the key, status and page variables in your query function!\nfunction fetchTodoList({ queryKey }) {\n  const [_key, { status, page }] = queryKey\n  return new Promise()\n}\n```\n\n## Using a Query Object instead of parameters\n\nAnywhere the `[queryKey, queryFn, config]` signature is supported throughout React Query's API, you can also use an object to express the same configuration:\n\n```js\nimport { useQuery } from 'react-query'\n\nuseQuery({\n  queryKey: ['todo', 7],\n  queryFn: fetchTodo,\n  ...config,\n})\n```\n"}
